#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111835 :tutorial/content "The **midpoint** of a line segment is the point that splits it into two segments of equal length. For example, $M$ is the midpoint of $\\overline{PQ}$ shown below.\n\n![](images/ed/ede582389ad936b02a6074a3fb6543e730ae955baf9784147f133f9492a35708.png)\n\nThe midpoint splits the line segment into two congruent segments,\n\n$$\n\\overline{PM}\\cong \\overline{QM}\n$$\n\nIn addition, the segment $\\overline{PQ}$ is twice as long as the segments $\\overline{PM}$ and $\\overline{QM}$:\n\n$$\nPQ = 2PM, \\qquad PQ = 2QM\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "9e476e67-9dd4-5098-be96-fc805e9e97f9" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2165 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111857 :tutorial/content "Let's find the midpoint $M$ of the line segment $\\overline{AB}$ on a number line, if $A$ corresponds to $x_1= 3$ and $B$ corresponds to $x_2=7$, as depicted below.\n\n![](images/11/115797e11eb3e434d5faefe412a8cefea216b6190436d408d199ac31d0da7210.png)\n\nThe midpoint of a line segment splits it into two segments of equal length.\nTo find the number $x_m$ corresponding to $M$, we use the following **midpoint formula**:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{m} &= \\frac{x_{1} + x_{2}}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x_1=3$ and $x_2=7$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{m} &= \\frac{3 + 7}{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{10}{2} \\\\\n&= 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the midpoint $M$ corresponds to $x_m=5$.\n\n![](images/fc/fc0a47734756e12ec117db92d8f0273ae564c03ead88ce86eac82005be6feaf4.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "7b14d3c0-0c84-55bc-be21-3359fbc5f6ec" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2347 :tutorial/title "The Midpoint Formula"}]}
